Modification: The panel's answer is asked again in the form the memory can check, in `solver/agent.py` and `solver/answer_space.py` only. A panel that agreed with itself — a split panel has no answer of its own to review — is asked the question once more with the memory's demonstrated answers and the panel's answer in the prompt, and returns the complete answer: keep every answer the panel gave, add a demonstrated answer only where the question clearly requires one the panel left out. The review is itself a panel under the same ceiling, wall, unanimity stop and failure tolerance, and its reading replaces the panel's answer only through `extends_answer`: every answer the panel gave survives whole, everything that appears besides them is a string the memory demonstrates as an answer, and the review panel agreed with itself too. A review that dies, is refused, runs out of wall, or answers anything else leaves the panel's answer exactly as the panel left it, and an answer space that never joins several answers into one (no separator) has nothing to add to, so those samples are not reviewed at all. Grounding, the vote, the ladder, REFUSALS_PER_RUNG, PANEL_CEILING, PANEL_MINIMUM, PANEL_SETTLE, PANEL_FAILURES, memory preparation and the retrieval rendering are unchanged.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3167, "Symptom2Disease": 0.9, "LawBench": 0.57, "average": 0.5956, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The expected effect was not achieved: the mechanism ran exactly as designed and added nothing. 89 of the 100 LawBench samples ran a review — 0 of the 60 USPTO and 0 of the 100 Symptom2Disease samples, whose answer spaces never join several answers into one — and the review panel's reading repeated the panel's answer in 87 of those 89, so no sample's answer was changed by an accepted extension. The two reviews that differed proposed a rewrite of a charge the grounding had mangled, which `extends_answer` refuses by construction ([[LawBench/conversations/0030/sample-0.json]] answers "故意伤害;帮助毁灭证据" against the mangled "故意伤害;帮助毁灭证"), and the four LawBench answers that differ from the parent's are two of those and two on a contested panel that was never reviewed. Every per-task count is identical to the parent (19/60, 90/100, 57/100; 0.5956 against 0.5956) and the two within-task flips are the panel's own churn: LawBench example 7 both samples (10 calls each, no review, so the panel itself moved) and USPTO's usual one in and one out. What the run does establish is negative and measured: putting the memory's whole demonstrated answer list — 193 strings — in front of the model and asking for the complete answer does not make it add a demonstrated answer the panel did not already write, so LawBench's under-prediction is not a recall failure of the harness's evidence but the model's reading of the case; and the review panel's own unanimity stop fired on only 28 of the 89 reviews, so it ran deep on the rest and paid for draws that agreed on nothing — an exact-string vote is the wrong stop for a question that re-lists an answer. The cost is certain and was paid: model calls 1210 -> 1935 (+709 on LawBench, 4.60 -> 11.69 per sample) and input tokens 29.9M -> 49.5M (LawBench 23.5M -> 43.0M), with LawBench's mean elapsed 71s -> 156s and its max 278s -> 421s, inside the wall."}

